In an oil refinery, a Fluid Catalytic Cracking Unit (FCC) converts heavy gas oils into high-value gasoline blending components.

The feed to an FCC may or may not be hydrotreated. The FCC was designed with a specific type of feed. FCC feed can be divided between hydrotreated and non-hydrotreated.

The term "hydrotreated" means the gas oil was processed in a Hydrotreating unit. In a hydrotreater, hydrogen is reacted with the feed under high-pressure and in the presence of a catalyst. The hydrotreating process removes sulfur, nitrogen, and metal contaminants such as nickel and vanadium.

When feed to the FCC is hydrotreated, there are at least three major impacts:

✅ Improved FCC product yields because of the addition of hydrogen.

✅ Lower FCC catalyst consumption (operating costs) because of lower nickel and vanadium. Both nickel and vanadium poison (deactivate) catalyst.

✅ Eliminates the need to install flue gas desulfurization (which requires a lot of capital). The hydrotreater lowers the feed sulfur before it gets to the FCC.

